    description: "**Defect (residual of WI-ritaf).** `packages/hypergumbo-lang-rust-analyzer/recorded/aardvark_dns/crate/` is a recorded fixture crate (a copy of aardvark-dns consumed by `packages/hypergumbo-lang-rust-analyzer/tests/test_recorded_fixtures.py` and the recorded rust-analyzer replay), but neither `selection.filters.is_test_path` nor `paths.is_test_file` classifies `recorded/` as test or test-support. \n\n**Evidence.** With WI-ritaf's `profile.languages[L].test_files` (branch jgstern-agent/feat/ritaf-language-test-files), a self-profile of this repo (origin/dev af8ff42ad0 + that branch) reports `rust: files=19 test_files=5`; the 14 non-test rust files are all under `packages/hypergumbo-lang-rust-analyzer/recorded/aardvark_dns/crate/`. So rust still reads as a production language of hypergumbo (which has no first-party Rust), while the other fixture-only languages now read `test_files == files`.\n\n**Consequence.** The composition summary WI-ritaf fixed still overstates the stack by one language on this repo; the same path also feeds ranking/slice test filtering via the shared predicate.\n\n**Constraint.** `is_test_path` / `is_test_file` are shared chokepoints (ranking, slice, linker filtering, sketch). Adding `recorded/` there changes every consumer; it may be better scoped as a repo-specific exclusion or a narrower fixture-directory rule. Measure the blast radius before changing the predicate. Not measured: whether any corpus repo uses a `recorded/` directory for production code."  # a0d4
    fields: {}  # a0d4
